WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:02.601
-Our next guest
is a talented actress

00:00:02.601 --> 00:00:04.400
who made her debut in
the Disney+ series "Ms. Marvel."

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.100
She returns as Kamala Khan
in the new movie, "The Marvels,"

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:09.601
which is in theaters now.
Let's take a look.

00:00:09.601 --> 00:00:10.934
-Whoa!

00:00:10.934 --> 00:00:12.400
-Unh!

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:13.834
-Friends of yours?

00:00:13.834 --> 00:00:15.834
-Hi.

00:00:15.834 --> 00:00:25.834
♪♪

00:00:25.834 --> 00:00:31.200
♪♪

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:32.667
-Unh!

00:00:32.667 --> 00:00:37.467
♪♪

00:00:37.467 --> 00:00:40.801
-Please welcome back to the show
Iman Vellani, everybody.

00:00:40.801 --> 00:00:42.567
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:42.567 --> 00:00:52.567
♪♪

00:00:52.567 --> 00:00:56.534
♪♪

00:00:56.534 --> 00:00:58.734
-I am so happy you're back,
Iman.

00:00:58.734 --> 00:01:00.100
-I'm so glad to be here.

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:01.968
-That is such a fantastic clip.

00:01:01.968 --> 00:01:04.501
And I watch a clip like that,
and I think, "Oh, my God.

00:01:04.501 --> 00:01:07.567
That probably is like
two days of work."

00:01:07.567 --> 00:01:09.834
-I think when
it's really me, one.

00:01:09.834 --> 00:01:11.734
-Okay.
-I have a CG double.

00:01:11.734 --> 00:01:13.634
-There's a -- oh, CG double.
Okay.

00:01:13.634 --> 00:01:14.868
-Yeah, she's great.
-Can you --

00:01:14.868 --> 00:01:16.067
You can't do
the full barrel roll

00:01:16.067 --> 00:01:17.701
in the sky like that?
-Can you?

00:01:17.701 --> 00:01:19.734
-No, that's true.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:19.734 --> 00:01:20.834
How dare you put me
in my place like that?

00:01:20.834 --> 00:01:22.267
-That's two things
we have in common.

00:01:22.267 --> 00:01:24.133
[ Laughter ]
-Before we get to the movie,

00:01:24.133 --> 00:01:25.767
I'm so --
I was so excited to hear --

00:01:25.767 --> 00:01:27.267
You were watching
"The Sopranos."

00:01:27.267 --> 00:01:29.534
-Yeah.
-And I'm so happy

00:01:29.534 --> 00:01:30.801
when younger people
find that show.

00:01:30.801 --> 00:01:32.968
And then it broke my heart
when I realized

00:01:32.968 --> 00:01:35.000
it came out
before you were born.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:36.901
-Uh-oh.
-Yeah.

00:01:36.901 --> 00:01:38.667
I'm not the only one
who's bummed out by that fact.

00:01:38.667 --> 00:01:40.968
-2002, baby.
-2002, baby.

00:01:40.968 --> 00:01:42.801
Are you enjoying it?
-I am.

00:01:42.801 --> 00:01:43.901
Like, every time
I watch a new show,

00:01:43.901 --> 00:01:45.901
I, like, kind of take
that personality.

00:01:45.901 --> 00:01:50.701
I'm like, "Tony and Adriana,
you got all that from gabagool?"

00:01:50.701 --> 00:01:52.634
Like, it's just so good.

00:01:52.634 --> 00:01:54.868
-They must be so happy
they finished filming the movie.

00:01:54.868 --> 00:01:56.334
[ Laughter ]

00:01:56.334 --> 00:01:58.434
You didn't show up to set and
you're like, "I got a new way

00:01:58.434 --> 00:02:00.701
I'm gonna do the character."
-I know.

00:02:00.701 --> 00:02:02.467
No, I've been --
I've been pretty productive.

00:02:02.467 --> 00:02:05.334
-Yeah. You -- Another thing
that's very fun for me.

00:02:05.334 --> 00:02:08.534
Every time we talk about you
as Ms. Marvel

00:02:08.534 --> 00:02:11.100
is that you dressed --
Before you were Ms. Marvel,

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:13.534
you dressed up like her
on Halloween.

00:02:13.534 --> 00:02:14.701
-Yeah.
[ Audience "Awws" ]

00:02:14.701 --> 00:02:16.033
-I mean, that's amazing.
-My grandma helped

00:02:16.033 --> 00:02:18.367
sew this whole thing together.
-That's fantastic.

00:02:18.367 --> 00:02:20.334
-Now I got a whole department
for that.

00:02:20.334 --> 00:02:21.734
-And you still continued to --

00:02:21.734 --> 00:02:25.334
You are a Marvel fan.
You were Tony Stark one year.

00:02:25.334 --> 00:02:26.767
[ Laughter ]

00:02:26.767 --> 00:02:29.067
And here you are
as Jubilee.

00:02:29.067 --> 00:02:31.400
Is Jubilee recent? Is that?
-That is. Okay.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:34.367
So, I've, like, been really
into "X-Men" lately.

00:02:34.367 --> 00:02:35.968
I am actually writing
a comic book.

00:02:35.968 --> 00:02:37.200
-I know.
We're gonna get to that.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:38.334
-Okay.
Sorry, I'm spoiling it.

00:02:38.334 --> 00:02:40.701
I needed to do a lot
of "X-Men" research,

00:02:40.701 --> 00:02:41.868
and I never really read
those comics

00:02:41.868 --> 00:02:43.167
because they're so intimidating.

00:02:43.167 --> 00:02:45.133
There's a million of them.
-Yes, there are, yes.

00:02:45.133 --> 00:02:47.934
So I watched, like, the "X-Men"
'90s cartoon in the background.

00:02:47.934 --> 00:02:50.200
I had, like, Grant Morrison's
"New X-Men" on my laptop.

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:52.100
And then I was, like, reading
Jonathan Hickman's new "X-Men."

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:54.367
It was, like, so much "X-Men"
all at once,

00:02:54.367 --> 00:02:55.801
and I just had to dress up.

00:02:55.801 --> 00:02:58.200
-Now, when you dress up
in costume,

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:00.868
I know you've done it as sort
of events where fans are there.

00:03:00.868 --> 00:03:02.234
Do they recognize
you right away,

00:03:02.234 --> 00:03:03.968
or can you sort of
remain anonymous?

00:03:03.968 --> 00:03:06.267
-You can, like, see
the gears turning.

00:03:06.267 --> 00:03:08.767
I mean, I was wearing a mask,
which is, like, still nice,

00:03:08.767 --> 00:03:10.400
but you stand out more
because people are like,

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:11.734
"COVID still exists."

00:03:11.734 --> 00:03:13.634
-Oh, you're wearing
that kind of mask?

00:03:13.634 --> 00:03:15.200
We are talking
about superheroes,

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:17.734
so forgive me for thinking
this kind of mask.

00:03:17.734 --> 00:03:19.701
[ Laughter ]

00:03:19.701 --> 00:03:20.934
So, this is -- this is --

00:03:20.934 --> 00:03:23.601
I'm so -- I'm so impressed
that you did this.

00:03:23.601 --> 00:03:26.267
This is your comic
that you wrote, and, uh...

00:03:26.267 --> 00:03:27.634
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:27.634 --> 00:03:29.767
Four issues.
Three are out, one to go.

00:03:29.767 --> 00:03:31.767
-One to go.
-And how have you enjoyed

00:03:31.767 --> 00:03:33.400
the process of writing
your own comic book?

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:36.033
-It's the most
special thing ever.

00:03:36.033 --> 00:03:38.000
I can't be on your show
without bringing up Sana Amanat.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:41.100
She's the reason this happened.
She co-created the character,

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:43.667
and she made a couple calls
to Marvel editorial

00:03:43.667 --> 00:03:45.267
and forced them
to let me write it.

00:03:45.267 --> 00:03:48.434
And I really don't know what she
said to them, but it's real.

00:03:48.434 --> 00:03:50.167
And that's my last name,
which is so cool.

00:03:50.167 --> 00:03:52.133
-Yeah.
-It's great.

00:03:52.133 --> 00:03:53.534
We were just talking about
how cool it is

00:03:53.534 --> 00:03:55.234
to receive art every morning.
-Yes, 'cause you just

00:03:55.234 --> 00:03:57.467
write a script,
and then you send it off,

00:03:57.467 --> 00:03:58.968
and then, like,
drawings come back.

00:03:58.968 --> 00:04:01.133
You cannot believe it.
-Yeah, it's either the most

00:04:01.133 --> 00:04:03.234
validating thing when they,
like, write --

00:04:03.234 --> 00:04:04.868
they draw exactly
what you wrote,

00:04:04.868 --> 00:04:06.567
or you're like, "Okay.

00:04:06.567 --> 00:04:09.501
Maybe I need to fix
my writing a little bit."

00:04:09.501 --> 00:04:11.000
-Did you have
any hard conversations

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:12.934
where you realized, not that
they did something wrong,

00:04:12.934 --> 00:04:14.901
but you wrote it
in a way that was unclear

00:04:14.901 --> 00:04:16.400
and they did something right,

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:18.334
but you got it wrong, and you
had to be like, "I'm so sorry"?

00:04:18.334 --> 00:04:19.601
-I'm just gonna say
I'm wrong because they're

00:04:19.601 --> 00:04:21.334
probably going to watch this.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:04:21.334 --> 00:04:22.534
I think it's always the best.

00:04:22.534 --> 00:04:24.300
I mean, again, if you --
they're so talented

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:25.701
that if they draw something
the wrong way --

00:04:25.701 --> 00:04:27.767
That's what we found.
I wrote one with Bill Hader.

00:04:27.767 --> 00:04:29.267
-I know.
-Oh, you do?

00:04:29.267 --> 00:04:31.968
-I read it, like,
right before coming here.

00:04:31.968 --> 00:04:35.167
It is like, you can imagine
two dudes writing that comic.

00:04:35.167 --> 00:04:37.934
[ Laughter ]
It's like -- It's like --

00:04:37.934 --> 00:04:39.701
I mean that in the best way
possible.

00:04:39.701 --> 00:04:41.734
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:41.734 --> 00:04:43.200
But, okay --
But listen to the story.

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:45.934
-It's like -- It's like
Spider-Man, and then --

00:04:45.934 --> 00:04:48.434
and then a dude dressed up
as Spider-Man on Halloween,

00:04:48.434 --> 00:04:50.467
and he's, like, wasted.
Okay?

00:04:50.467 --> 00:04:52.434
And then the two
of them switch places,

00:04:52.434 --> 00:04:54.067
and the bad guy thinks he got
Spider-Man,

00:04:54.067 --> 00:04:58.167
but he got the drunk Spider-Man.
It's -- It's great.

00:04:58.167 --> 00:05:03.167
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:05:03.167 --> 00:05:05.167
-By the way, I'm --
You might -- I'm --

00:05:05.167 --> 00:05:08.534
This is the most anyone's
ever talked about our issue.

00:05:08.534 --> 00:05:10.100
This is the best thing
that's ever happened to me.

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:11.167
-I'm glad
I'm putting it on the map.

00:05:11.167 --> 00:05:12.934
-Yeah. And guess what --
joke's on you.

00:05:12.934 --> 00:05:16.067
Two dudes did write it.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:16.067 --> 00:05:18.534
Hey, I was surprised to hear
that Billy Joel

00:05:18.534 --> 00:05:19.701
was an inspiration
in any way,

00:05:19.701 --> 00:05:21.567
shape, or form
for your comic-book writing.

00:05:21.567 --> 00:05:23.767
-Yes, I am obsessed
with "River of Dreams."

00:05:23.767 --> 00:05:26.767
It's like my one of my favorite
albums, my favorite song.

00:05:26.767 --> 00:05:28.868
And it's like --
he very much explores,

00:05:28.868 --> 00:05:30.601
you know, where we go in --

00:05:30.601 --> 00:05:35.634
I mean, it's a play on stream of
consciousness and dealing with

00:05:35.634 --> 00:05:38.601
our inner demons
and, you know, finding yourself.

00:05:38.601 --> 00:05:41.601
And I think that's a lot of what
Kamala's journey is in my book.

00:05:41.601 --> 00:05:44.801
She's a mutant now, and that's
a whole new label that she

00:05:44.801 --> 00:05:47.300
has to adapt to.
And, you know,

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:49.667
the entire world's perspective
kind of shifts on her

00:05:49.667 --> 00:05:52.033
now that she has this label
and her own --

00:05:52.033 --> 00:05:54.133
how she perceives herself
has shifted.

00:05:54.133 --> 00:05:57.033
So, yeah, we literally put her
in her own dreams,

00:05:57.033 --> 00:05:59.834
and she has to fight off her
fears and doubts. It's great.

00:05:59.834 --> 00:06:01.200
-That's fantastic.
I think you're probably

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:02.901
one of the few people
that uses Billy Joel

00:06:02.901 --> 00:06:05.000
to write your mutant comics.
-Oh, God.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:06.234
[ Laughter ]

00:06:06.234 --> 00:06:08.367
I hope he serenades me
one day with that song.

00:06:08.367 --> 00:06:10.400
[ Laughter ]

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:12.801
-We got to see, in the clip,
that you got to work

00:06:12.801 --> 00:06:15.601
with the great Sam Jackson.
What is it like?

00:06:15.601 --> 00:06:18.367
Because again, you're not
just a wonderful performer.

00:06:18.367 --> 00:06:20.367
You are also a fan
of these films.

00:06:20.367 --> 00:06:23.033
And Sam Jackson, obviously, not
just in the Marvel Universe

00:06:23.033 --> 00:06:25.667
but in the entire cinematic
universe is a big deal.

00:06:25.667 --> 00:06:28.234
What was he like?
-What was he like?

00:06:28.234 --> 00:06:29.300
Um...
[ Laughter ]

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:30.434
Every time I stare at him,

00:06:30.434 --> 00:06:32.400
this like 16x9 frame
would appear around him.

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:35.167
It was the weirdest thing.

00:06:35.167 --> 00:06:37.167
He's a very generous
scene partner,

00:06:37.167 --> 00:06:38.567
a very encouraging
scene partner.

00:06:38.567 --> 00:06:41.300
I was doing that fight scene
with him, actually,

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:45.000
and he -- I wanted
to try something new,

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:48.067
so I high-fived him
at the end of the scene.

00:06:48.067 --> 00:06:50.801
-Right.
-Creative choices, people.

00:06:50.801 --> 00:06:52.868
And then -- And then we cut,
and Sam goes,

00:06:52.868 --> 00:06:54.467
"They won't use that take."

00:06:54.467 --> 00:06:56.100
[ Laughter ]

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:58.300
"Do you know why
they won't use that take?"

00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:01.300
And I was like...

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:03.667
And he's like, "Your hand
was covering your face."

00:07:03.667 --> 00:07:05.934
-Ahh!
-And I was like, "Oh, my God.

00:07:05.934 --> 00:07:08.767
I'm so sorry
I ruined this for us!"

00:07:08.767 --> 00:07:10.634
And he's like, "Okay.
On the next one, elbow me."

00:07:10.634 --> 00:07:12.734
And I was like, "What?"
He's like, "Yeah, elbow me."

00:07:12.734 --> 00:07:14.234
I was like, "Okay."
So we get to the end

00:07:14.234 --> 00:07:16.067
of the scene, and I, like,

00:07:16.067 --> 00:07:17.734
so scared of touching
Sam Jackson.

00:07:17.734 --> 00:07:20.300
I like lightly elbowed him,
and then we cut.

00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:24.367
He's like, "No, no no no!
Elbow me!"

00:07:24.367 --> 00:07:26.033
And I was like,
"Okay, I'm sorry!"

00:07:26.033 --> 00:07:28.067
We do another take.
I fully chickened out.

00:07:28.067 --> 00:07:30.734
Could not figure out
the maneuvering of an elbow.

00:07:30.734 --> 00:07:32.434
Did not do it, and he goes --

00:07:32.434 --> 00:07:36.300
He grabs my arm and, like,
turns to me, and he's like,

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:39.968
"Elbow me, mother [bleep]!"
And I was like, "Listen..."

00:07:39.968 --> 00:07:43.868
[ Laughter, cheers, applause ]

00:07:43.868 --> 00:07:46.467
For the record,
he didn't actually swear at me.

00:07:46.467 --> 00:07:49.067
That's just how I heard it.

00:07:49.067 --> 00:07:51.801
I was trying to get him
to swear at me for so long.

00:07:51.801 --> 00:07:53.467
-He wouldn't do it.
-Nothing but hugs.

00:07:53.467 --> 00:07:55.400
-Yeah, nothing but hugs.
[ Audience "Awws" ]

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:57.234
I'm so glad you gave us

00:07:57.234 --> 00:07:59.234
the multiverse version
of Sam Jackson.

00:07:59.234 --> 00:08:00.734
[ Laughter ]

00:08:00.734 --> 00:08:03.467
You told the story where he did
call you a mother [bleep].

00:08:03.467 --> 00:08:05.801
-Yeah, it was a dream.
-It was a dream.

00:08:05.801 --> 00:08:06.934
I love that those
are your two dreams --

00:08:06.934 --> 00:08:09.400
"Oh, my God. If Billy Joel
would serenade me

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:11.501
while Sam Jackson
called me a mother [bleep]."

00:08:11.501 --> 00:08:14.234
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:08:14.234 --> 00:08:16.567
"What more could a kid
ever ask for?"

00:08:16.567 --> 00:08:17.934
-I want that on my gravestone.

00:08:17.934 --> 00:08:19.334
-Yes, exactly.

00:08:19.334 --> 00:08:23.434
Hey, you know, because you are
informed on this universe,

00:08:23.434 --> 00:08:24.734
I heard there's times on set

00:08:24.734 --> 00:08:26.968
where you actually
catch something

00:08:26.968 --> 00:08:29.133
that maybe doesn't
make perfect sense.

00:08:29.133 --> 00:08:31.868
-Yes. I've e-mailed
our director, Nia DaCosta,

00:08:31.868 --> 00:08:33.901
like, a laundry list
of plot holes,

00:08:33.901 --> 00:08:35.601
and, like,
"I don't think Sam Jackson

00:08:35.601 --> 00:08:37.868
would actually say this,

00:08:37.868 --> 00:08:39.434
but here's what
he could say instead."

00:08:39.434 --> 00:08:41.033
I'm sure
she very much appreciated

00:08:41.033 --> 00:08:43.100
all of that unsolicited advice.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:45.167
But, no, there was, like,
literally one day on set,

00:08:45.167 --> 00:08:47.634
I pointed something out really
early while we were filming it,

00:08:47.634 --> 00:08:48.934
and I'm like,
"This doesn't make sense."

00:08:48.934 --> 00:08:50.234
And they were like,
"Iman, just like, chill.

00:08:50.234 --> 00:08:53.100
Sit down for one second."

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:55.434
And then, like,
half the day goes by,

00:08:55.434 --> 00:08:57.167
and then suddenly
Nia notices.

00:08:57.167 --> 00:08:58.868
"Hey, this actually
doesn't make sense."

00:08:58.868 --> 00:09:00.901
And then Teyonah goes,
"No, you're right."

00:09:00.901 --> 00:09:03.100
And I'm sitting there.

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:04.901
"It's almost like someone
told you."

00:09:04.901 --> 00:09:05.901
[ Laughter ]

00:09:05.901 --> 00:09:07.801
Um, so, yeah, I only --

00:09:07.801 --> 00:09:10.033
-So you were cool about.
-I was cool about it, totally.

00:09:10.033 --> 00:09:11.300
It's not in the movie
anymore, either, so...

00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:13.300
-All right.
Well, you were right again.

00:09:13.300 --> 00:09:16.100
And it is just so wonderful
to have you back here.

00:09:16.100 --> 00:09:18.234
I am so thrilled that
the strike is over

00:09:18.234 --> 00:09:21.133
so you can be out promoting
this wonderful film

00:09:21.133 --> 00:09:24.567
and your wonderful comic.
You guys, that's Iman Vellani.

00:09:24.567 --> 00:09:26.667
"The Marvels"
is in theaters now.

00:09:26.667 --> 00:09:28.067
Her comic book is on the racks.

00:09:28.067 --> 00:09:30.300
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

